    outlook: "The immediate future hinges on de-escalation efforts by international actors and the responses of the involved parties. Diplomatic channels through the United Nations and regional powers are likely to intensify. However, further retaliatory strikes remain a significant risk. Investors will closely watch oil production figures from OPEC+ nations and any statements from the US Federal Reserve regarding inflation control. The conflict could also become a significant issue in upcoming national elections in various countries, including the United States, influencing voter sentiment on foreign policy and economic management. Any clear signs of de-escalation or, conversely, further military action will be the primary catalysts for market movements in the coming weeks.",
    conclusion: "The recent escalation in the US-Israel-Iran conflict serves as a stark reminder of the fragility of global peace and the interconnectedness of world economies. As oil prices surge and markets reel, the humanitarian cost and the threat to international stability cannot be overstated. A concerted, diplomatic effort is urgently needed to avert a wider catastrophe, protecting not only regional lives but also the economic well-being and future prospects of nations across the planet.",
